            <ArticleTitle>Torsade de pointes induced by short-term oral amiodarone therapy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1051-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Although amiodarone appears to have few pro-arrhythmic effects, torsade de pointes (TdP) has been observed during long-term drug administration, usually in conjunction with electrolyte disturbances, a change in drug dosage, or concomitant drug therapy. We report two cases of amiodarone-induced TdP shortly after administration of a low dose of oral amiodarone, in the absence of predisposing factors.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
